<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Praetorian Prefecture of Africa</h1><p class="st-meta">534 CE – 591 CE (approx.) · capital Carthage</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Justinian's government for reconquered North Africa, set up at Carthage after the fall of the Vandals.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom</a> in 533 to 534, Justinian created a Praetorian Prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. It covered the former Roman provinces from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Mauretania</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corsican-republic">Corsica</a>, under a civil prefect and a separate military commander.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, taxation and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene</a> church came with confiscation of Arian property. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, who also served as prefect, built fortresses across <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/numidia">Numidia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byzacena">Byzacena</a>, many still visible today. The new regime faced army mutinies, notably that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stotzas">Stotzas</a> in 536, and repeated wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> powers such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-capsus">Kingdom of Capsus</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/laguatan">Laguatan</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-troglita">John Troglita</a> restored order around 548.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>To strengthen defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, the emperor Maurice combined civil and military authority under an exarch around 591. The prefecture was thus replaced by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/exarchate-of-africa">Exarchate of Africa</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> remained as subordinate officials.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/praetorian-prefecture-of-africa.jpg" alt="Praetorian Prefecture of Africa" width="320" height="182"><figcaption>Image: Ichthyovenator · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exarchate_of_Africa_600_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>534 CE – 591 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Carthage</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Solomon, John Troglita</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chalcedonian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Byzantine Africa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=562&amp;focus=praetorian-prefecture-of-africa" aria-label="Praetorian Prefecture of Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4939 850 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Praetorian Prefecture of Africa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5259 1106L5259 1106M5259 1106L5709 970M5259 1106L5259 1106"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 1106h0M5709 970h0M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Carthage. <a href="/atlas?year=562&amp;focus=praetorian-prefecture-of-africa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
